seems a good discard: it's a simple tile, not connected, and likely safe because W suits haven't been heavily discarded by others.\n\nCheck safety: Previous player discarded T1 and T8, not W. Next player discarded B3, not W. Across player discarded W8, so they might have some W tiles, but W6 is not necessarily dangerous.\n\nAlternatively, discard F4: honor tile, but F4 has been discarded by previous player, so might be safe. But keeping F4 might allow a wind pung if I draw another, but F4 is not prevailing/seat, so less points.\n\nI'll rank discards: \n1. Play W6: safe, breaks no pairs, low utility.\n2. Play T5: safe, breaks no pairs, low utility.\n3. Play F4: honor, but possibly safe.\n4. Play T2: similar to T5.\n\nI think W6 is the best.\n\nReason: W6 is an isolated simple tile with no strong connections in my hand, making it the least useful for forming melds. Discarding it maintains pairs and potential chows (like W1-W2 waiting for W3, or B4-B5 waiting for B3/B6). It is also relatively safe based on discard history, as no player has shown strong interest in W suits. Keeping more connected tiles like B5 or singles that can pair with existing tiles (e.g., W1 with W2) offers better flexibility for future draws.\n\nAnswer: Play W6"}
